WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:01.067
Jake Sullivan.

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.068
Most war.

00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:05.739
Almost all wars end
in some kind of a negotiation.

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:10.977
The sort of World War two war
where one side complete only annihilates.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:13.680
The other is very, very rare in history.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:16.716
What will that that end?

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.785
What will those negotiations look like?

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:21.788
The Chinese have put out this.

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.490
I don't know what they're call it,
a peace plan.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.893
It's just out it's a 12 point document
detailing its position,

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:30.730
calling for the end of hostilities
and the resumption of peace talks.

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:32.599
Is there anything to this?

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.334
What is your reaction?

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:37.504
Well, my first reaction to it
is that it could stop at point one,

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:40.006
which is respect
the sovereignty of all nations.

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:42.409
That's the first point in the 12 point
plan.

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:46.479
This war could end tomorrow
if Russia stopped attacking Ukraine

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:49.115
and withdrew its forces.
Ukraine wasn't attacking Russia.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.617
Naito wasn't attacking Russia.

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:52.886
The United States wasn't attacking Russia.

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:55.855
This was a war of choice
by Putin waged upon Ukraine.

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.958
And it could end
if he simply left Ukraine.

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:01.928
And that is the best way for this war
to end.

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:04.397
Now, I cannot predict the future.

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:07.100
What I can tell you
is that the United States

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.203
is not going to dictate
to Ukraine how this war ends.

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:13.740
President Biden tells President Zelensky
and our allies

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.010
at every opportunity
nothing about Ukraine without Ukraine.

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:23.316
It is the Ukrainians who will decide how
they proceed towards the end to this war.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:27.720
Our job is to put them in the best
possible position on the battlefield.

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:30.523
So they are in the best possible position

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:34.861
to be able to do diplomacy
whenever they choose to do diplomacy.

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:36.496
And that is how we are going to proceed.

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:40.767
But I think there's one more important
point as we approach this anniversary

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.202
and is actually the anniversary

00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:45.905
has arrived in Ukraine, and that is
Russia has already lost this war.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:49.909
Russia's aims in
this war were to wipe Ukraine off the map,

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:54.747
to take the capital and to eliminate
Ukraine, to absorb it into Russia.

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:56.549
They failed at doing that,

00:01:56.549 --> 00:02:00.320
and they are in no position
to be able to do that as we go forward.

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:03.623
And it is important for everyone
to remember that the courage and bravery

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:06.459
of the Ukrainian people
has already accomplished that objective

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:09.963
and the support of the United States
and our allies and partners around

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:12.165
the world has helped contribute to that.

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:13.900
But where this goes from here

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.336
is something that will play out
over the coming months.

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:20.140
What we know is day by day
We simply have to keep doing our job,

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.209
which is to give the Ukrainians
the tools they need to defend themselves.

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:26.679
So whatever
happens at the end of this war,

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:30.950
Ukraine is going to need a massive amount
of reconstruction and assistance.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.087
Does the world have the capacities?

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:37.891
The United States have the capacity
for the kind of thing you know,

00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:43.129
people talk about a Marshall Plan
like there was after 1945 to help Europe

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:43.997
rebuild.

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:46.533
Is that the kind of scale
we need to be thinking about?

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:50.937
Well, I think we associate
the Marshall Plan with a moment, right,

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.108
just as we associate
the end of the war or two with a moment.

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:59.646
And it looks as though recovery
and reconstruction

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:03.082
in Ukraine, at least for the time
being, is going to happen differently.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:08.021
Just this month, earlier
this month, the world Bank announced

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:11.658
$50 million to invest in the repair
and restoration

00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:15.328
of the transport networks,
the transport infrastructure in Ukraine.

00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:16.563
And that's how U.S.

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:20.366
aid is proceeding as we try to catalyze
the involvement of the European Bank

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:22.035
for Reconstruction Development.

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:25.772
The big international financial
institutions and to get the private sector

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:26.306
to be interested

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:29.142
in coming back to those parts of Ukraine
that are relatively peaceful

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:33.379
and even to continue to make investments
elsewhere.

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:37.784
We just struck
a Public-Private partnership with Bayer,

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:41.421
which is building a new seed factory
in Ukraine, which is going to employ

00:03:41.421 --> 00:03:43.056
thousands of Ukrainians.

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:44.924
The more Ukrainians are employed,

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:48.962
the more revenue there is the less
assistance will need to come from outside.

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:51.130
So this is going to be from within
and from without.

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:54.067
But I think it's not necessarily
going to be an on off switch.

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:57.870
It's something we want more
Ukrainian refugees to be in a position

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:01.040
to come home to have infrastructure
that awaits them

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.843
where they can live in buildings
that have been repaired.

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:08.181
But, of course, the damage
that some estimates is that the damage

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:13.052
so far has been $130 billion if you take
arable land, homes, hospitals, schools.

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:15.688
So this is going to be
a mammoth undertaking.

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:19.792
The other thing we want to do now
is with an eye to those big ticket items,

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:24.097
which most of which will happen
only when there is a negotiated peace.

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:25.798
But we have to

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:28.434
make sure that resources are going to be
well spent.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.837
When you have those huge investments

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.006
which go well beyond
what is being provided right

00:04:33.006 --> 00:04:34.874
now, that's when, of course,
you want to make sure

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:39.912
that you have the safeguards in place
so that all outside investors and donors

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:42.649
know that and can say to their citizens

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.651
that this is money
that's going to be well spent.

00:04:44.651 --> 00:04:48.921
But I think President Biden has spoken
really eloquently, powerfully

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:53.192
to how much enthusiasm there will be
when this war is officially over.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.262
You know,
a number of actors are on the outside

00:04:56.262 --> 00:04:59.532
really wanting to be a part of the longer
term solution.

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:03.269
But getting the institutional frameworks
right is something we can be working on

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:03.770
right now.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.039
In addition to these stopgap recovery
efforts.

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:08.308
Jake, I got to ask you one final question.

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:11.110
We've talked all about the world,
Russia, Ukraine.

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:14.247
We haven't talked about
what's going on in the United States.

00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:20.887
Do you worry when you hear voices
like Governor DeSantis, Senator Hawley,

00:05:22.288 --> 00:05:23.523
Senator Vance

00:05:23.523 --> 00:05:27.627
questioning why the United States is doing
this, asking why we should be spending

00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:30.063
this money, wondering whether we should be

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:32.999
taking a more neutral position

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:36.436
What I find so interesting

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:38.471
about that perspective, we can't

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:42.842
operate in the world
because we have to operate at home is it

00:05:42.842 --> 00:05:47.447
presents a fundamentally false choice
that is not at all who America is.

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:50.149
We can both invest at home

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:53.419
and provide for the safety
and well-being of the American people.

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:54.954
And we can lead in the world.

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:57.290
And that's what we have done at our best

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:00.193
under Democratic
and Republican presidents for decades.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.131
The United States is capable
as a powerful self-assured nation.

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:07.200
We have the resources,
we have the talents,

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:10.837
we have the energies of our people
to solve our own problems here.

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:15.408
And President Biden has done more
in two years to invest in this country,

00:06:15.408 --> 00:06:18.644
to build jobs,
to provide for the social safety net,

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:21.881
to deal with the problems that people sit
around their kitchen tables

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:27.487
and think about while at the same time
mobilizing a coalition of free nations

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:30.390
to support the values
that Americans hold so dear.

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:34.727
So what I would say to those senators
is, yes, let's do these things at home.

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:38.564
But are you saying
that America is incapable of also

00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:42.835
helping to serve a powerful force
for good in the world?

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:46.272
I don't think that the American people
believe that.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:49.976
I think the American people think
we are capable of doing both.

00:06:50.176 --> 00:06:53.179
And at our best,
that is exactly what we have done.

00:06:53.179 --> 00:06:57.650
And I believe that a lot of the moments
I've seen in this last year in Ukraine,

00:06:57.917 --> 00:07:00.720
from those flags waving in small towns
that Samantha

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.756
was talking about
to the people in the U.S.

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:06.859
government who are trying to support
folks like you

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:10.329
who are on the front lines,
that has been America at its best.

00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:13.733
And so I think that there's a pessimism
in this argument

00:07:13.733 --> 00:07:15.535
that these senators are making.

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:19.906
President Biden has an optimistic view,
which is we can do it and we should do it

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:22.442
and we are doing it. And as a result,

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:26.779
I believe that democracies in the world
are getting stronger, not weaker,

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.916
as the president said, and autocracies
are getting weaker, not stronger.

00:07:30.116 --> 00:07:32.952
And that is better
for every single person in this country.

